{"causes": ["The flourishing intellectual environment of Al-Andalus under Islamic rule fostered cross-cultural exchange and advanced scholarship in philology and science.", "Earlier Hebrew grammarians and Masoretes had identified linguistic patterns but lacked a comprehensive, systematic explanation for all verbal forms, particularly those involving 'weak' letters.", "The need for a rigorous, scientific approach to biblical exegesis and the preservation of Hebrew as a living scholarly language motivated deep linguistic inquiry among Jewish scholars."], "effects": ["Hayyuj's identification of the triliteral root system became the undisputed foundation for all subsequent Hebrew grammar, standardizing linguistic analysis for centuries.", "His work enabled more accurate and consistent interpretation of biblical texts, profoundly influencing Jewish scholarship and exegesis across the diaspora.", "The philological methods he pioneered were adopted and expanded by later grammarians like Jonah ibn Janah, cementing the scientific study of Hebrew."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "judah-ben-david-hayyuj", "sourceName": "Judah ben David Hayyuj", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "kitab-al-afal-dhawat-huruf-al-lin", "targetName": "Kitab al-Af'al Dhawat Huruf al-Lin", "context": "Judah ben David Hayyuj authored the foundational text 'Book of Verbs Containing Weak Letters' around 990 CE, establishing his theory of triliteral roots."}, {"sourceSlug": "al-andalus", "sourceName": "Al-Andalus", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "judah-ben-david-hayyuj", "targetName": "Judah ben David Hayyuj", "context": "Judah ben David Hayyuj conducted his groundbreaking linguistic research and resided in Al-Andalus, specifically in Cordoba, during the late 10th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "judah-ben-david-hayyuj", "sourceName": "Judah ben David Hayyuj", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "jonah-ibn-janah", "targetName": "Jonah ibn Janah", "context": "Jonah ibn Janah, a leading 11th-century gramma